mmc: dw_mmc: handle an all-ones register read as "host gone", not as work

Two places treat a 0xffffffff read from an absent controller as meaningful:

1. dw_mci_wait_while_busy() polls SDMMC_STATUS with readl_poll_timeout_atomic()
   for up to 500ms before every data command - atomic, host->lock held, irqs
   disabled. All-ones means SDMMC_STATUS_BUSY is permanently set, so every
   command burns the full 500ms and a retrying driver pins the CPU.

2. dw_mci_interrupt() reads MINTSTS and, if non-zero, services it. All-ones
   means it "handles" all 32 bits, the writes to RINTSTS go nowhere, and since
   the line is level triggered it is re-entered immediately.

(2) is the more damaging: measured at ~690,000 interrupts/second on a Gusgu H7
(RK3326) when the RK915 SDIO wifi chip drops off the bus under concurrent load.
Per-irq tracing during the hang:

    IRQTRACE 32 timer=57321/62722/55965/64999 dwmci: 54:3363849 vop=6888 adc=13510
    IRQTRACE 33 timer=57627/63111/56569/65405 dwmci: 54:4053907 vop=6888 adc=13510

irq 54 is mmc@ff380000 (the sdio host). Every other CPU0-affine interrupt is
frozen: the VOP stops (drm vblank timeouts), the SARADC stops (joypad error
spam), the network dies. RCU then stalls because the CPU is permanently in irq
context entered from idle, so it never reaches an extended quiescent state -
the stall report shows it in default_idle_call() looking idle, which is
misleading; it is being re-interrupted between every WFI.

An all-ones read is not data. Bail out in both places: let the command time out
normally so the error reaches the driver, and return IRQ_NONE so the spurious
irq machinery can shut the line down.

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c	2026-08-27 16:48:54.029757320 -0400
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c	2026-08-27 16:48:54.030904680 -0400
@@ -213,11 +213,26 @@
 	 */
 	if ((cmd_flags & SDMMC_CMD_PRV_DAT_WAIT) &&
 	    !(cmd_flags & SDMMC_CMD_VOLT_SWITCH)) {
+		/*
+		 * An all-ones read means the controller is not answering at all
+		 * - unclocked, powered down, or the card has fallen off the bus
+		 * - it does not mean "busy". Treating it as busy makes this
+		 * atomic poll burn its full 500ms with host->lock held and
+		 * interrupts disabled, and a caller that keeps retrying then
+		 * pins the CPU: interrupts stop being serviced, RCU stalls, and
+		 * the machine wedges hard enough that printk stops coming out.
+		 * Break out instead and let the command time out normally, so
+		 * the error reaches the driver and its recovery path can run.
+		 */
 		if (readl_poll_timeout_atomic(host->regs + SDMMC_STATUS,
 					      status,
+					      status == ~0U ||
 					      !(status & SDMMC_STATUS_BUSY),
 					      10, 500 * USEC_PER_MSEC))
 			dev_err(host->dev, "Busy; trying anyway\n");
+		else if (status == ~0U)
+			dev_err_ratelimited(host->dev,
+					    "host not responding (status=~0)\n");
 	}
 }
 
@@ -2674,6 +2689,24 @@
 
 	pending = mci_readl(host, MINTSTS); /* read-only mask reg */
 
+	/*
+	 * An all-ones read means the controller is not answering - unclocked,
+	 * powered down, or the card fell off the bus - not that every interrupt
+	 * is pending. Servicing it "handles" all 32 bits, the writes to RINTSTS
+	 * go nowhere, and because the line is level triggered we are re-entered
+	 * immediately: a ~690k/s storm that starves every other interrupt on the
+	 * CPU. On a Gusgu H7 that stops the VOP (vblank timeouts), the SARADC
+	 * (joypad error spam) and the network, and RCU stalls because the CPU is
+	 * permanently in irq context entered from idle - the machine appears to
+	 * hang. Return IRQ_NONE so the spurious-irq machinery can shut the line
+	 * down instead of the CPU spinning on it forever.
+	 */
+	if (pending == ~0U) {
+		dev_err_ratelimited(host->dev,
+				    "host not responding, MINTSTS=~0; ignoring\n");
+		return IRQ_NONE;
+	}
+
 	if (pending) {
 		/* Check volt switch first, since it can look like an error */
 		if ((host->state == STATE_SENDING_CMD11) &&
